---
// The vetting rules, as their own page.
//
// This material was briefly printed on the landing page, which was the wrong
// place for it twice over: it buried a sparse design under prose, and body
// copy under a wordmark cannot rank for the questions it actually answers.
// Here it has a title and a URL of its own, so "is flow formed the same as
// forged" can land on the paragraph that answers it.
//
// It is also the only content on this site that is argument rather than data,
// which makes it the page most likely to be linked to or quoted, and links
// are the thing the sourcing sheets cannot generate for themselves.
import { loadWheels } from "../lib/data.js";
import "../styles/global.css";

const wheels = loadWheels();
const supplierCount = wheels.reduce((n, w) => n + w.suppliers.length, 0);

const canonical = new URL("/methodology/", Astro.site).href;
const description =
  `How WheelSourced vets Chinese wheel factories: one reference spec, ` +
  `quoted prices only, and why flow forming is never recorded as forging.`;

const breadcrumbs = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: new URL("/", Astro.site).href },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Methodology", item: canonical },
  ],
};
---

<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
<title>WheelSourced - Methodology</title>
<meta name="description" content={description} />
<link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
<meta property="og:type" content="article" />
<meta property="og:site_name" content="WheelSourced" />
<meta property="og:title" content="WheelSourced - Methodology" />
<meta property="og:description" content={description} />
<meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
<meta name="twitter:title" content="WheelSourced - Methodology" />
<meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbs)} is:inline />
<link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/favicon-32.png" sizes="32x32" />
<link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/favicon-192.png" sizes="192x192" />
<link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any" />
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Barlow+Condensed:wght@600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
<style>
  .doc { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 20px 80px; }
  .doc h1 {
    font-family: 'Barlow Condensed';
    font-size: clamp(32px, 6vw, 48px);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 40px 0 8px;
  }
  .doc .sub {
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono';
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--soft);
    margin: 0 0 36px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .doc h2 {
    font-family: 'Barlow Condensed';
    font-size: 24px;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin: 40px 0 10px;
  }
  .doc p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--soft); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .doc p b { color: var(--ink); }
  .doc .back {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono';
    font-size: 11.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bronze);
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="sheet">
  <header class="masthead">
    <a class="logo" href="/" aria-label="WheelSourced home">
      <span class="word">Wheel<em>Sourced</em></span>
    </a>
  </header>
</div>

<div class="doc">
  <h1>How the vetting works</h1>
  <p class="sub">{supplierCount} suppliers &middot; {wheels.length} wheels &middot; updated continuously</p>

  <p>Every factory listed here was contacted directly and asked the same
    questions about the same reference specification. What follows is what we
    do with the answers, including the answers we do not get.</p>

  <h2>One reference spec, so the prices compare</h2>
  <p>Quotes are requested at a single specification, the same one for every
    factory. A price for a different construction is not converted or adjusted
    to fit the column; it is set aside and the question is asked again. Two
    numbers only belong side by side if they answer the same question.</p>

  <h2>Flow forming is not forging</h2>
  <p>"Rotary forged" and "flow formed" describe a <b>cast centre with a spun
    barrel</b>. It is a legitimate process and it produces a decent wheel, but
    it is not forging, and a supplier who answers the forging question with it
    is recorded as having described flow forming.</p>
  <p>The reverse matters just as much. "Machined from a forged blank" is
    ordinary forging and is recorded as forged. Suppliers routinely answer
    this question in the negative, too, because we ask them to: "fully forged,
    not flow-formed" is a claim of forging, not a confession of it.</p>

  <h2>Construction and forging are separate questions</h2>
  <p>Whether a wheel is forged and how many pieces it has are independent.
    A <b>cast three-piece is an honest product</b> and is not marked down for
    being one, and a forged monoblock is not "fewer pieces" as though that
    were a defect. Neither field is ever inferred from the other, and the
    number of constructions a factory can build is recorded as a capability
    rather than being narrowed to whichever one they last quoted.</p>

  <h2>Missing answers are published as missing</h2>
  <p>When a supplier does not answer a question twice, the sheet says
    <b>not disclosed</b>. Nothing is estimated, inferred or filled in to make
    a row look complete, and a blank is never quietly treated as a zero.</p>
  <p>That rule exists because the opposite has a direction. Landed cost was
    once computed by treating an unquoted freight charge as nothing, which
    made the factory that withheld its shipping cost look like the cheapest on
    the page. <b>Landed cost is now shown only when both the wheel price and
    the freight were quoted</b>, so declining to answer can never look better
    than answering.</p>
  <p>The same principle runs the other way: a supplier is never penalised for
    telling us more. One who quotes a single all-in figure covering wheels,
    shipping and duties has answered more completely than one who quotes ex
    works, and is recorded that way rather than being dropped from the
    comparison for refusing to break it down.</p>

  <h2>Quoted figures and listing figures are kept apart</h2>
  <p>Every value carries its origin. Figures a factory gave us directly are
    printed in black; figures taken from a platform listing are printed grey
    and labelled. The two are never merged, and a listing claim is never
    allowed to stand in for an answer to a question. Certifications are shown
    only as the supplier stated them, because listings overclaim and rendering
    an unvetted list beside a short honest one would reward the overclaim.</p>
  <p>Naming a certification and having sent the certificate are also separate
    facts. A supplier who says they hold DOT approval but sends nothing is
    recorded as having claimed it without a document, and a supplier who says
    plainly that they cannot supply one is recorded as having said so, which
    is a useful and honest answer rather than a failure.</p>

  <h2>What the score is, and what it is not</h2>
  <p>The vetting score is out of 100 and combines store rating, store age,
    review count, response time, reorder rate and transit time. Every axis is
    judged against a fixed standard rather than against the other factories in
    the set, so a young shop is thin whether or not a rival is thinner.</p>
  <p>A rating counts for as much as its sample supports: 5.0 from two buyers
    is not a better rating than 4.8 from eighty-seven, only a less certain
    one, so ratings are weighted by review count before scoring. The result is
    scaled by how many axes a factory actually has, so a sparse record cannot
    outrank a complete one on a lucky average. Expect real numbers in the 40s
    to 60s.</p>
  <p><b>Price is deliberately not part of the score.</b> "Cheapest" and "most
    trustworthy" are different questions, and scoring them together meant a
    factory's trustworthiness moved when a competitor happened to quote low.
    Price is shown as its own figure and drives its own sort.</p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <p>We do not sell wheels and we do not take payment from the factories for
    placement. We do not give fitment advice, because whether a wheel clears
    your particular car is a judgement we are not positioned to make. And we
    describe wheels by construction and geometry rather than as another
    maker's product, which is both more accurate and more honest about what a
    factory is actually offering to build.</p>

  <a class="back" href="/">&larr; Back to the sourcing sheets</a>
</div>
</body>
</html>
